                <pb facs="0987" n="673" sameAs="frus:frus1914Supp/pg673"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 811.741/22a</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Germany</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Gerard</hi>)</head>
                <dateline>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                    <placeName>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                        calendar="gregorian" when="1914-08-19T14:00:00-05:00">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">August 19, 1914, 2 p.m.</hi>
                    </date>
                </dateline>
                <p rendition="#center">[Telegram]</p>
                <p>Please inquire of the Foreign Office whether the German Government has itself any
                    connection, direct or indirect, with the radio station at Sayville, Long Island,
                    now operated by a corporation known as the Atlantic Communication Company, or
                    with the radio station at Tuckerton, New Jersey, built by the Hoch Frequenz
                    Maschinen Actiengesellschaft für Drahtlose Telegraphic. We feel bound in
                    fairness to the German Government to make these inquiries for our guidance in
                    performing our obligations under the third article of the Hague convention of
                    1907.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">W. J. Bryan</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
